<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#agraharam">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agraharam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agrahara, Chaturvedimangalam, Sāsana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An agraharam or agrahara was a grant of land and of the royal income from it, typically made by a king or noble family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> for religious purposes, particularly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> to maintain temples or a pilgrimage site and to support their families. Dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, Kadambas, Pallavas and Vijayanagara built and maintained them; they were also called Chaturvedimangalams, and Sāsana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. By traditional town-planning an agraharam was two rows of houses on either side of a road, with a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> at one end and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> at the other. An early description appears in the 3rd-century CE Sangam work Perumpāṇāṟṟuppaṭai, and many places in southern India bear the name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agraharam.jpg" alt="Agraharam" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Enforcer Office of the secret service · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhaskararajapuram_agraharam.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brahmin settlements called <b>agraharas</b> and temple mathas served as schools for Sanskrit learning, logic and law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
